        <!-- ═══ SECTION 1 ═══ -->
        <h2 id="why-wheel-works">
          1. Why a Wheel Segment Outperforms Scripted Content
        </h2>

        <p>
          The wheel's core advantage over almost every other content device is
          simple:
          <strong>nobody involved controls the result</strong>. In a landscape
          where audiences are increasingly good at spotting manufactured
          spontaneity, something that's visibly, mechanically random stands out
          precisely because it can't be faked convincingly.
        </p>

        <ul class="uc-list">
          <li>
            <div class="uc-icon twitch">⚡</div>
            <div class="uc-body">
              <strong>Nobody knows what happens next — including you</strong>
              <span
                >Each spin is a small plot twist the creator experiences in real
                time alongside the audience. That shared uncertainty is doing a
                lot of the engagement work a well-produced segment usually has
                to fake.</span
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="uc-icon youtube">📺</div>
            <div class="uc-body">
              <strong
                >Unscripted reactions travel further than scripted ones</strong
              >
              <span
                >The clips that circulate best are genuine reactions to genuine
                surprises. A wheel landing on an outcome the creator visibly
                didn't want produces exactly that, without needing to be
                engineered.</span
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="uc-icon teal">🤝</div>
            <div class="uc-body">
              <strong>It turns spectators into contributors</strong>
              <span
                >Once viewer-submitted content is on the wheel, or chat is
                voting on segments, the audience has a stake in the outcome.
                That small sense of authorship is what turns a one-time viewer
                into someone who returns for the next spin.</span
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="uc-icon gold">🔁</div>
            <div class="uc-body">
              <strong>It becomes a format, not a one-off</strong>
              <span
                >A recurring wheel segment develops its own history — outcomes
                that never land, inside jokes about specific segments, running
                community bets. That accumulated context is what turns "a wheel"
                into "the wheel," a recognisable part of your channel.</span
              >
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="uc-icon orange">📊</div>
            <div class="uc-body">
              <strong>It gives long sessions a rhythm</strong>
              <span
                >Unstructured streams bleed viewers steadily because there's no
                reason to keep watching right now. A wheel creates small,
                regular events with their own build-up and payoff — enough to
                keep someone who was about to close the tab around for one more
                spin.</span
              >
            </div>
