        <!-- ═══ SECTION 2 ═══ -->
        <h2 id="formats">2. Six Wheel Formats Creators Actually Use</h2>

        <p>
          Most wheel content on streaming and video platforms falls into one of
          six recognisable formats. Knowing the mechanics of each makes it much
          easier to pick the one that actually fits your content and audience,
          instead of forcing a format that doesn't.
        </p>

        <div class="creator-grid">
          <div class="creator-card cc-twitch">
            <div class="creator-emoji">🎮</div>
            <div class="creator-platform">Twitch / YouTube Live</div>
            <h3>The Game Randomiser</h3>
            <p>
              The wheel sets gameplay conditions — character, difficulty,
              restriction, required item. The challenge is finishing the session
              under whatever the wheel imposed, not completing a separate dare.
              It works across nearly every genre and gets more intense as
              conditions compound.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="creator-card cc-youtube">
            <div class="creator-emoji">🎯</div>
            <div class="creator-platform">YouTube / Short-Form</div>
            <h3>The Subscriber Dare Wheel</h3>
            <p>
              Subscribers submit dares through comments, forms, or chat; the
              creator loads the strongest submissions and spins live. Because
              the audience wrote the content, every outcome carries a little
              more weight — and the submitter gets a moment of visible credit.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="creator-card cc-twitch">
            <div class="creator-emoji">❤️</div>
            <div class="creator-platform">Charity Streams</div>
            <h3>The Donation Milestone Wheel</h3>
            <p>
              Segments trigger as fundraising hits each threshold — the higher
              the tier, the bigger the outcome. This ties the wheel directly to
              the donation total, giving supporters a concrete reason to push
              past the next milestone.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="creator-card cc-youtube">
            <div class="creator-emoji">🗳️</div>
            <div class="creator-platform">Both Platforms</div>
            <h3>The Audience Vote Wheel</h3>
            <p>
              Chat votes on what belongs on the wheel, or which segments get
              cut, while the creator curates for quality and safety. Strong for
              milestone streams — anniversaries, sub-count goals — where
              community ownership should feel front and centre.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="creator-card cc-twitch">
            <div class="creator-emoji">🏆</div>
            <div class="creator-platform">Tournament / Collab</div>
            <h3>The Elimination Wheel</h3>
            <p>
              Players, games, or outcomes compete across rounds, with the wheel
              deciding match-ups. As entries get eliminated, the wheel shrinks
              and each remaining spin carries more weight — a natural fit for
              multi-creator streams and tournament brackets.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="creator-card cc-youtube">
            <div class="creator-emoji">🛒</div>
            <div class="creator-platform">YouTube / Sponsorships</div>
            <h3>The Mystery Item Wheel</h3>
            <p>
              Numbered products, foods, or unboxings are loaded onto the wheel,
              and the spin decides what's experienced next. The randomisation
              solves the predictability problem that flat, ordered unboxing
              videos run into.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- ═══ SECTION 3 ═══ -->
        <h2 id="gaming">3. Gaming Randomiser Wheels, in Detail</h2>
        <div class="platform-badge badge-both">🎮 Twitch + YouTube</div>

        <p>
          The gaming randomiser earns its versatility because it works across
          nearly every genre — the wheel doesn't bolt a dare onto the game, it
          changes the game itself. Here's how the strongest variants are built:
        </p>

        <h3>Character or loadout randomiser</h3>
        <p>
          Load every playable character, class, or loadout onto the wheel and
          spin at the start of each match or run. The restriction is immediate,
          visible, and holds for the whole session — building a running question
          ("can they actually win with this?") that tends to hold attention
          better than clean, skilled gameplay on its own.
        </p>
        <p>
          Strongest fits: fighting games, battle royales, class-based RPGs, hero
          shooters, and deck-builders. The bigger the gap between the best and
          worst loadout in the game, the higher the stakes of each spin.
        </p>

        <h3>Restriction stack wheel</h3>
        <p>
          Each spin adds one more permanent restriction to the session, starting
          clean and stacking: "pistols only," then "no crouching," then "must
          taunt after every kill." By spin four or five the accumulated
          restrictions make the game nearly unplayable — which is precisely
          where this format peaks.
        </p>

        <figure>
          <img
            src="/wheel-settings.webp"
            alt="A gamer at a battle-station setup laughing in disbelief at a monitor showing a spin wheel that landed on an absurd restriction, colourful RGB lighting in the background"
            loading="lazy"
            width="640"
            height="360"
          />
        </figure>

        <div class="pill-list">
          <span class="pill pill-purple">🔫 Pistols only</span>
          <span class="pill pill-twitch">🧍 No crouching</span>
          <span class="pill pill-yt">🙈 No looking at the minimap</span>
          <span class="pill pill-gold">🎭 Must taunt after every kill</span>
          <span class="pill pill-teal">🏃 Never stop moving</span>
          <span class="pill pill-orange">🔇 No audio allowed</span>
          <span class="pill pill-purple">🎯 Only headshots count</span>
          <span class="pill pill-twitch">🔢 One life only</span>
          <span class="pill pill-yt">🕶️ Eyes closed during reloads</span>
          <span class="pill pill-gold"
            >🤝 Compliment every opponent in chat</span
          >
        </div>

        <h3>Game roulette — what plays next</h3>
        <p>
          Load 8–12 titles from your library, then spin at the end of each
          session or on a set timer to decide what plays next. This suits
          variety streamers well — it gives every game on the wheel a genuinely
          fair shot instead of the streamer defaulting to whatever's
          comfortable.
        </p>

        <div class="box box-twitch">
          <div class="box-label">💜 Twitch-specific tip</div>
          <p>
            Tie channel points to specific segments on a game-roulette wheel.
            Viewers can spend points to "protect" a game they want played
            (temporarily pull it off the wheel) or "curse" one to appear twice.
            It gives the wheel a layer of audience agency that keeps chat active
            through the spin.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3>Speedrun category randomiser</h3>
        <p>
          Pair speedrunning with a category randomiser for some of the
          highest-tension wheel content available. Load Any%, 100%, Low%,
          Glitchless, and Random Item Only alongside difficulty and time-limit
          modifiers. Every spin produces a specific, demanding target with a
          visible finish line, which structures the whole session around one
          question: can this actually be completed?
        </p>

        <!-- ═══ SECTION 4 ═══ -->
        <h2 id="subscriber-dares">4. Subscriber Dare and Challenge Wheels</h2>
        <div class="platform-badge badge-youtube">▶ YouTube</div>

        <p>
          The subscriber dare wheel is closely tied to viral YouTube content,
          and it holds up consistently because the mechanic is simple: the
          audience writes the challenge, the creator lives it. Because nothing
          was scripted, the creator's reaction is real.
        </p>

        <h3>Sourcing dares that actually work</h3>
        <ul class="step-list">
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong
                >Ask for something specific, not "dares" in general</strong
              >
              <p>
                "Submit a gaming challenge," "submit a food I have to eat,"
                "submit something I can do in this room right now." A narrow
                prompt produces sharper submissions; an open call mostly returns
                noise.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Curate hard before anything goes on the wheel</strong>
              <p>
                Out of a couple hundred submissions, maybe 10–15 belong on the
                wheel. Ask: will this produce a real reaction, is it completable
                in the time you have, is it safe and appropriate, and does it
                differ from what's already on there.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>State your completion rules before the first spin</strong>
              <p>
                "I'll attempt every dare once. If it's genuinely not possible, I
                take a penalty instead." Saying this upfront heads off any sense
                that you're quietly picking and choosing which outcomes to
                honour.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Credit the submitter out loud</strong>
              <p>
                "This one's from [username]" before you complete it. It gives
                the submitter a personal reason to clip and share the moment —
                and across a video with 10–15 dares, that adds up to real reach.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Turn on remove-after-spin</strong>
              <p>
                Each dare gets completed exactly once, and the shrinking wheel
                naturally raises the stakes as it goes. Save your most dramatic
                submission as a visible final segment so the audience is
                anticipating it the whole way through.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Sample dare wheel for gaming creators</h3>
        <div class="pill-list">
          <span class="pill pill-yt"
            >🎮 Beat the next level on max difficulty</span
