        <!-- ═══ SECTION 7 ═══ -->
        <h2 id="youtube-specific">
          7. Formats That Work Especially Well on YouTube
        </h2>
        <div class="platform-badge badge-youtube">▶ YouTube</div>

        <p>
          YouTube videos have to hold attention across 10–20 minutes without
          live energy to lean on, so the wheel needs to earn its place in the
          edit differently than it does on stream. A few formats consistently
          deliver:
        </p>

        <h3>"I let the wheel decide my [X] for a week"</h3>
        <p>
          The wheel picks something to live with for seven full days — diet,
          workout plan, sleep schedule, outfit choices, gaming rotation — and
          the video documents what happens. The extended commitment, not the
          spin itself, is what carries the video: day-one uncertainty, mid-week
          adjustment, day-seven payoff. The wheel is just the spark.
        </p>

        <h3>Blind taste test or mystery unboxing wheel</h3>
        <p>
          Number a set of foods, products, or mystery boxes and spin to decide
          what gets opened next. This solves the predictability problem plain
          unboxing videos run into — when viewers can guess the order, the
          reveal loses its punch. A wheel makes every reveal genuinely
          uncertain.
        </p>

        <h3>Comment-sourced challenge compilation</h3>
        <p>
          Pin a comment asking for submissions, compile the best after a week,
          and film the completion video. This arrives with a built-in audience —
          everyone who submitted is likely to watch, and several will share it
          if their idea makes the cut.
        </p>

        <h3>Versus wheel — two creators, one wheel</h3>
        <p>
          Both creators' names go on the wheel; the spin decides who completes
          the next challenge, with the other's reaction as the secondary payoff.
          The dynamic between the two — watching a collaborator get landed on
          the worst outcome — is usually what carries collab content, not the
          challenge itself.
        </p>

        <div class="box box-yt">
          <div class="box-label">▶ YouTube title and thumbnail note</div>
          <p>
            Titles that name the stakes outperform vague ones: "I Let a Wheel
            Decide My Diet for 7 Days (I Regret This)" does more work than
            "Wheel Challenge Video." Pair a visible wheel with a clear reaction
            expression in the thumbnail — the wheel is the promise, your face is
            the proof it was kept.
          </p>
        </div>

