        <!-- ═══ SECTION 8 ═══ -->
        <h2 id="build">8. Wiring the Wheel Into OBS or Streamlabs</h2>

        <ul class="step-list">
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Build the wheel before you go live</strong>
              <p>
                Open <a href="/" class="ilink">spinthewheelsonline.com</a>,
                enter every segment, match the colours to your stream branding,
                and run a full test spin. Never build a wheel live on camera for
                the first time — the setup drains the energy the wheel is meant
                to add.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Add it as a browser source</strong>
              <p>
                In OBS or Streamlabs, add a Browser Source and paste the wheel's
                URL. This captures the live wheel — spin animation and sound
                included — directly into your scene. Size the source to match
                its role: full-screen for wheel-focused segments, a smaller
                overlay for background use during gameplay.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Give it a dedicated scene</strong>
              <p>
                Build a scene with just the large wheel and your face cam, and
                switch to it for every spin. The clean transition signals to
                viewers that something distinct is about to happen and gives the
                wheel the visual space it deserves.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Route the wheel's audio into your mix</strong>
              <p>
                Make sure the browser source's audio is captured by OBS. The
                ticking deceleration sound carries a lot of the suspense — test
                the levels beforehand so it doesn't drown out your mic, but
                don't leave it muted either.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong>Bookmark the URL as a backup</strong>
              <p>
                The wheel's URL holds your full configuration. Save it as a
                browser bookmark and note it in your OBS scene notes — if the
                source breaks mid-stream, reloading takes under 30 seconds
                instead of losing the segment entirely.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
          <li>
            <div class="step-num"></div>
            <div class="step-body">
              <strong
                >For recorded video, film the spin as a separate take</strong
              >
              <p>
                If you're editing rather than streaming live, filming the spin
                on its own gives you full control over the pacing of the reveal
                and lets you capture your reaction separately for a cleaner cut.
                The result is still genuinely random either way.
              </p>
            </div>
          </li>
        </ul>

