        <h2 id="mistakes">
          10. Where Creators Undercut Their Own Wheel Content
        </h2>

        <p>
          The wheel itself is simple. Where it goes wrong is almost always in
          how it's run.
        </p>

        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>Loading outcomes you won't actually honour.</strong> The
            whole format rests on the audience believing the result is binding.
            The first time a creator re-spins a result they didn't like, or
            quietly "can't" do the outcome that landed, the wheel loses its
            meaning. Set the rules before spinning, then follow them without
            exception.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Making every segment roughly equal.</strong> A wheel with
            genuine contrast — some outcomes the creator clearly wants, some
            they dread — is what produces real, visible reactions. A wheel where
            nothing much is at stake produces flat content. Every good wheel
            needs at least one segment that gets a nervous laugh as the wheel
            slows down near it.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Spinning too fast to let outcomes land.</strong> Rushing
            through five spins in two minutes gives each result no room to
            breathe. Treat every outcome as its own complete moment — set it up,
            complete it, let the audience react — before moving to the next
            spin.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Not showing the full wheel before the first spin.</strong>
            An audience that can see every possible outcome can calculate odds
            and anticipate results, which raises the stakes of every subsequent
            spin. A 30-second scroll through the segment list before spinning is
            worth the time.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Only reaching for the wheel when content stalls.</strong>
            Using it purely as a filler tool trains the audience to see it as a
            last resort. It performs best as a scheduled feature with its own
            dedicated time, not something pulled out reactively when nothing
            else is happening.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Forgetting to credit audience contributions.</strong> If a
            segment came from a viewer, reading their name before the outcome
            isn't just courtesy — it's the mechanism that turns individual
            viewers into people actively sharing your content with their own
            networks.
          </li>
        </ul>

