        <h2 id="faq">11. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Can I add a spin wheel to OBS as a browser source?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Yes — this is the standard way to bring an interactive wheel onto
            your overlay. Click <strong>Embed Wheel</strong> in the Save &amp;
            Share card to copy a clean, ad-free <code>?embed=1</code> URL (or
            share link), add a Browser Source in OBS Studio or Streamlabs, and
            set your overlay dimensions. It renders at high DPI with sound
            effects enabled, and you can pair it cleanly with a fullscreen or
            scene-specific layout.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Can viewers accuse me of rigging the result?<span class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Spin The Wheels Online draws every result using the browser's Web
            Cryptography API (<code>crypto.getRandomValues()</code>) with
            rejection sampling before the wheel animation begins—there is zero
            way for click timing to influence where it lands. Keep the full
            entry list visible before spinning, stick to every landing slice,
            and click Export CSV under Spin History for timestamped draw logs.
            See
            <a href="/blogs/is-spin-wheel-truly-random" class="ilink"
              >whether a digital spin wheel is truly random</a
            >.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            How many segments should a challenge wheel have?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            For live streaming, 8–12 segments tends to be the sweet spot —
            enough variety to avoid rapid repeats across a 2–3 hour session
            while keeping each outcome's odds high enough to land regularly. For
            YouTube dare videos, 8–10 lets every outcome get proper screen time.
            For charity streams with milestone unlocks, 6–8 progressively
            unlocked segments usually works best.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            What's the best way to collect subscriber dare submissions?<span
              class="faq-icon"
              >+</span
            >
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            In rough order of effectiveness: a pinned comment on your most
            recent video (high visibility, catches already-engaged viewers), a
            Community tab poll with pre-listed options, a linked form in the
            description or Discord for longer submissions, and live chat
            submissions during a dedicated window. Try to collect on the
            platform where the content itself lives rather than a secondary
            channel — the strongest submissions tend to come from people already
            watching, not people you had to redirect elsewhere.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Does wheel content work for small creators, or only large
            channels?<span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            It often performs better for small and mid-sized creators. Formats
            that spotlight individual viewers — dare wheels, name wheels, live
            chat voting — create a level of personal recognition that scales
            poorly past a few hundred thousand subscribers but builds strong
            community ties in the low thousands. A viewer whose submission lands
            on the wheel of a 1,000-subscriber channel becomes a genuine
            advocate; at much larger scale, the same moment is rarer but harder
            to make feel personal. Lean into audience-spotlight formats while
            your community is still small enough for that recognition to land.
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <div class="faq-q" onclick="toggleFaq(this)">
            Are there platform rules I need to follow with wheel
            challenges?<span class="faq-icon">+</span>
          </div>
          <div class="faq-a">
            Yes, and they apply to whatever's on the wheel, not the tool itself.
            Standard platform guidelines still cover any dare or challenge a
            segment produces — an outcome doesn't become acceptable just because
            a random spin selected it rather than the creator choosing it
            directly. Curate segments before loading them, and drop anything you
            wouldn't be comfortable completing on camera under normal
            circumstances — this matters especially for charity wheels where
            high donation tiers can tempt creators toward more extreme outcomes.
          </div>
        </div>


